+game, caused by a diagnostic.
+
+The pointer cannot be tested beforehand. The copy is now guarded by a structured
+exception handler around a single bounded `memcpy` with nothing to unwind, and
+the data is copied into our own buffer before anything downstream sees it. This
+is the only place in the codebase that uses that tool, and it is the right one.
+
+### The rest
+
+| Risk | What happens |
+|---|---|
+| Resolution or window change | aspect change resets the detector; a lock made against the old shape is not kept |
+| Camera cut, respawn, cutscene | continuity restarts, the candidate is not discredited |
+| Loading screen with no camera | `LOST` is reported, and does not decay into `SEARCHING` |
+| Long stall, alt-tab | the limiter re-anchors instead of emitting a burst of catch-up frames |
+| Deferred contexts accumulating | `destroy_command_list` is handled; v2 leaked these |
+| Partial buffer writes | stale bytes fail the camera tests; nothing downstream sees them |
+| Torn read of shared state | seqlock on both the command and camera blocks, reader retries |
+| Mismatched telemetry version | the block is refused rather than reinterpreted |
+
+The architectural point: **nothing in this pipeline reports success it has not
+earned.** A component that loses its footing reports that, and the features
+above it decline rather than running on data they cannot vouch for.
+
+---
+
+## 4. Visible quality
+
+### Where quality actually comes from
+
+In order of how much each is worth:
+
+1. **Correct motion vectors.** Everything else is secondary. This is why the
+   reprojection base layer exists, why per-object motion overrides it rather
+   than replacing it, and why estimated motion is never accepted for generated
+   frames.
+2. **Not fighting another temporal system.** Two accumulators on the same frames
+   produce smearing neither is responsible for. Already enforced between the
+   motion paths; the same rule has to extend to the game's own temporal AA when
+   upscaling arrives.
+3. **The interface out of the pipeline.** Anything generated over the top of the
+   HUD is an artifact by definition.
+4. **Knowing what you cannot trust.** The reactive mask is what keeps glass,
+   particles, rain and alpha-tested foliage from being treated as if they had a
+   single depth and a single motion.
+
+### The quality position today
+
+The image pass runs on camera-reprojected motion instead of optical flow. For
+the static world that is not an approximation — it is exact, and optical flow
+never was. For traffic and pedestrians it is worse than optical flow, which at
+least sees them, and that is why the hybrid exists: reprojection as the base,
+estimation filling in only where reprojection is blind.
+
+So the honest claim is **better on most of the frame, with a known weakness on
+independent movers, and a defined path to fixing it** — not "better everywhere".
+
+### What the tiers cost
+
+Dropping the validation pass to half or quarter resolution coarsens the
+confidence mask. The effect is slightly softer handling at the edges of moving
+objects and around transparency. It does not affect the motion vectors
+themselves, which stay per-pixel. That is why it is the first thing given up and
+frames are the last.
+
+---
+
+## Summary
+
+| Axis | Today | When generation lands |
+|---|---|---|
+| Latency | unchanged; limiter may improve it | +14 ms at 2x, +21 ms at 3x — stated in the interface |
+| Performance | detection ~0.1% of the naive cost, stops after lock | governed; quality degrades before frames do |
+| Stability | driver-memory read guarded, every loss path reports itself | interop is the main new risk; repro harness first |
+| Quality | exact for the static world, weak on movers, hybrid covers the gap | per-object motion closes it |
+
+The one thing this document will not claim is that generated frames are free.
+They cost latency, the cost is inherent, and the number belongs on screen next
+to the button that switches them on.
+
+---
+
+# The second pass
+
+Added after the question "is two-pass literally doing it twice, and can there be
+something in between?" — which turns out to be the right question, because the
+usual answer to both is wrong.
+
+## Why the two-pass look is real
+
+People report that two passes look *more real*, and they are not imagining it.
+Two separate things happen when a model runs on its own output:
+
+**The footprint widens.** One evaluation only sees a limited neighbourhood
+around each pixel. A second sees a neighbourhood of neighbourhoods, so structure
+on a larger scale gets treated — how light settles across a wall, not just how
+one brick edge looks. There is no single-pass setting that produces this. It is
+a receptive-field effect, and it is the part people recognise.
+
+**Nothing is pushed as hard.** Two gentle evaluations both stay inside the range
+the model handles well. One hard evaluation leaves it, and leaving it is what
+produces halos and the plasticky sheen.
+
+And one thing that goes wrong:
+
+**High frequencies feed back.** The second pass does not see the game's frame,
+it sees the first pass's output — which the model was never trained on. Fine
+detail the first pass got slightly wrong is real content as far as the second is
+concerned, so it gets sharpened, written into temporal history, and amplified.
+This is where two-pass setups actually fail in practice.
+
+The two good effects live at low spatial frequencies. The bad one lives at high
+spatial frequencies. They are separable, so this implementation separates them.
+
+## What is built
+
+**A continuous dial, 1.00 to 2.00.** Above 1.00 the second pass runs and its
+result is mixed in at that weight. 1.35 is a real position, not a rounded-down
+1. That is the "somewhere between one and two" the question asked for, and it is
+a blend weight rather than a fractional evaluation — you cannot run 35% of a
+neural network, but you can run all of it and use 35% of the answer.
+
+**The strength is solved for the mix, not halved.** Treating the effect as
+compounding, one evaluation at `j` takes the picture to `(1 + j)`; mixing the
+second in at weight `f` lands at `(1 + j)(1 + f·j)`. Setting that equal to the
+requested `(1 + i)` gives
+
+```
+f·j² + (1 + f)·j − i = 0
+```
+
+with exactly one non-negative root. At `f = 1` it reduces to the familiar
+`sqrt(1 + i) − 1`; at `f = 0` it reduces to `j = i`. So the dial is continuous at
+both ends and every position on it delivers the strength the player asked for.
+Getting this wrong — halving, or sending the full value twice — is why partial
+second passes normally come out either weak or overdriven. `test_passes.cpp`
+asserts the round-trip across the whole grid.
+
+**The cheap second pass blends a correction, not an image.** Under load the
+second evaluation moves to half resolution and contributes only its *difference*
+from its own input. Blending the half-resolution image would soften what the
+first pass resolved; blending its correction cannot, because the correction has
+nothing above the half-resolution band in it. So the widened footprint arrives
+and the feedback of fine detail does not, at roughly a third of the cost. On the
+axis people actually notice, this is not the inferior option — it is the safer
+one.
+
+**The cost does not scale with the dial, and the interface says so.** Mixing in
+20% still runs the whole pass. A slider that implied otherwise would be lying
+about what it costs.
+
+## Where it sits in the degrade ladder
+
+```
+motion mask detail  →  second pass to half res  →  second pass off  →  3x → 2x  →  off
+```
+
+Shrinking before removing is not a formality. At half resolution the second pass
+keeps the widened footprint — the reason anyone asked for it — and gives up only
+fine detail it should not have been touching. Removing it gives up the look
+entirely. Those are not the same size of loss, so they are not the same rung.
+
+Whatever the governor does, the requested *strength* is never reduced: dropping
+to one pass returns the full value to that pass. The picture does not get weaker
+when the machine gets busier, it gets less gently arrived at.
+
+## Video memory
+
+A second evaluation needs its own temporal history, and the history is the
+expensive part — several full-resolution buffers held across frames. This is the
+one part of the product that costs meaningful memory rather than meaningful
+time.
+
+The answer is not to predict the size. A prediction that is 30% low produces
+exactly the failure it was meant to prevent: an allocation that succeeds, pushes
+the adapter past its budget, and turns into stutter that looks like a driver
+problem. So there are two measurements and one live rule:
+
+- **Before.** `IDXGIAdapter3::QueryVideoMemoryInfo` reports a per-process budget
+  and current usage. The difference is genuinely how much room there is — it
+  already accounts for every other application on the machine, which no
+  prediction can. A conservative 640 MB placeholder is used only until there is
+  something real to measure, and 384 MB of headroom is left for the game, which
+  allocates on its own schedule and does not know we are here.
+- **After.** The instance's real size is the usage delta across creating it.
+  From that moment there is no estimate left in the decision and the interface
+  prints a measured number.
+- **Live.** If usage reaches 96% of the budget, the second instance goes
+  immediately, without waiting for the time-based governor. Being at the edge of
+  the budget means the driver is about to start moving allocations across the
+  bus, and every frame spent deciding is a frame spent stuttering.
+
+Which adapter is asked matters: reading adapter zero on a laptop can mean
+reading the integrated GPU while the game renders on the discrete one. The
+render device's LUID is used when it is known, and the status line says which of
+the two happened rather than quietly guessing.
+
+On a 16 GB card this is a non-question — which matches the expectation that VRAM
+is not the binding constraint. The accounting exists so that it is still not a
+question on an 8 GB card with a browser open, and so the number on screen is
+read rather than invented.
+
+## What it still will not do
+
+Two evaluations need two independent feature instances. Sharing one history
+means feeding that history its own output, which drifts. No provider reachable
+from here exposes that today, so the dial reports inert instead of pretending —
+and the Graphics page says which of the three reasons applies (no capability, no
+memory, no budget) rather than looking broken. Same rule as everything else in
+this product.
